use std
into test_cut.amni

Native checks for the Amni-Stitch cut export, run with `hcw run he/test_cut.amni`. They write each file format, parse it back and compare it with the geometry that produced it. Write compact Helium: if expressions instead of if blocks wherever possible, short bodies on one line, no repeated code. Lengths of the cut geometry are millimetres, a pattern's own lengths are centimetres. The cut export state is `CU` (`fmt` 0 DXF, 1 SVG, 2 PLT; `sheet` the preset index, 12 meaning custom with `cw` and `ch`; `margin`, `gap`, `grain`, `seam`, `layers`), the nested result is `CUI` (items), `CUS` (sheets) and `CUR` (primitives with `kind`, `layer`, `first`, `n`, `r`, `rot`, `sheet`, `tx`, `tn`), whose points are in `CUP` and whose text characters are in `CUB`. `cu_build()` nests the current pieces and `cu_file(out, s)` writes sheet s. `apply_preset(i)`, `draft_all()` and the options `O` come from st_draft. The points helpers `P`, `padd`, `psub`, `pmul`, `plen`, `pdist`, `pnorm` come from st_geom and are used as they are: never define a function that st_geom, st_cut or std already has, and never define the same helper twice.

```amni
import math;
import st_geom;
import st_body;
import st_draft;
import st_cut;
var FAIL = 0;
var CHK = 0;
fn check(c bool, what const byte[:]) { CHK += 1; if !c { FAIL += 1; print("FAIL ", what); } }
struct En { kind int, layer int, closed bool, first int, n int, r real, rot real, tx int, tn int }
struct Dx { sect int, endsec int, eof bool, units int, acad int, layers int, poly int, seq int, bad int }
struct Sv { w real, h real, vw real, vh real, groups int, ink int, fills int, dashes int }
struct St { loops int, paths int, circs int, texts int, perim real, x0 real, y0 real, x1 real, y1 real }
var EN En[>..];
var EP Pt[>..];
var TB byte[>..];
var DX = Dx { sect: 0, endsec: 0, eof: false, units: 0, acad: 0, layers: 0, poly: 0, seq: 0, bad: 0 };
var SV = Sv { w: 0.0, h: 0.0, vw: 0.0, vh: 0.0, groups: 0, ink: 0, fills: 0, dashes: 0 };
var PLD = 0;
var ST = St { loops: 0, paths: 0, circs: 0, texts: 0, perim: 0.0, x0: 0.0, y0: 0.0, x1: 0.0, y1: 0.0 };
```

`layer_of(name)` is 0 for "CUT", 1 for "SCORE", 2 for "ENGRAVE" and minus one for anything else.

`en_new(kind, layer, closed)` pushes an `En` onto `EN` with that kind, layer and closed, first equal to the length of `EP`, n 0, r 0, rot 0, tx 0 and tn 0, and returns its index. `en_pt(i, p)` pushes p onto `EP` and adds one to the n of `EN` entry i.

`fval(s)` is the real number that the text s holds after `trim`, by `parse_flt`, or 0.0 when it is not a number.

`attr(tag, name)` returns the slice of the text tag that lies between the double quotes after a space, then name, then an equals sign and a double quote (so the pattern ` name="`), up to the next double quote; an empty slice when the pattern is missing.

`parse_dxf(s, h)` clears `EN`, `EP` and `TB`, sets every field of `DX` to zero or false, and reads the DXF text s as pairs of lines, each pair being a group code line and then a value line, found with `split` on the newline byte. An empty last piece after the final newline is not a pair and is ignored. When the trimmed code line is not a whole number (`parse_int`) it adds one to `DX.bad` and skips the pair. It keeps the current section name (the value of a group 2 that follows a group 0 whose value is SECTION), the last group 0 value as the entity type, the last group 9 value as the header variable, the index of the entity being read (minus one for none), and the x just read. For every pair: group 0 with value SECTION adds one to `DX.sect`, ENDSEC adds one to `DX.endsec`, EOF sets `DX.eof`, LAYER adds one to `DX.layers`, SEQEND adds one to `DX.seq` and ends the entity being read, POLYLINE adds one to `DX.poly` and starts a path entity (`en_new` with kind 0, layer minus one, closed false), LINE starts a path entity, CIRCLE a kind 1 entity and TEXT a kind 2 entity, all three with layer minus one and closed false. In the HEADER section a group 70 after the header variable `$INSUNITS` sets `DX.units`, and a group 1 after `$ACADVER` sets `DX.acad` to the whole number after its first two letters. In the ENTITIES section: group 8 sets the layer of the entity being read through `layer_of`, unless the entity type is VERTEX or SEQEND; group 70 while the entity type is POLYLINE sets closed from bit one of the value; group 10 stores x; group 20 stores y turned into h minus y when h is above zero and then, for type VERTEX, LINE, CIRCLE or TEXT, pushes the point of x and that y with `en_pt` onto the entity being read; group 11 stores a second x and group 21 then pushes the point of that x and h minus the value as a second point of the LINE entity; group 40 sets r of the entity being read; group 50 sets its rot; and group 1 sets tx to the length of `TB`, appends the value to `TB` and sets tn to its length.

`parse_svg(s)` clears `EN`, `EP` and `TB`, sets every field of `SV` to zero, and walks the tags of the SVG text s, a tag being the text between a less-than sign and the next greater-than sign. For each tag: any occurrence of `fill="` that is not followed by `none"` adds one to `SV.fills`, and any occurrence of `dasharray` adds one to `SV.dashes`. A tag starting with `svg ` sets `SV.w` and `SV.h` from the numbers before the `mm` in its width and height attributes (`attr`), and `SV.vw` and `SV.vh` from the third and fourth numbers of its viewBox attribute. A tag starting with `g ` adds one to `SV.groups`, sets the current layer from its id attribute through `layer_of`, and adds one to `SV.ink` when its inkscape:groupmode attribute is `layer` and its inkscape:label attribute equals its id. A tag starting with `path ` starts a path entity on the current layer from the numbers of its d attribute: the d attribute is split on spaces; M and L are skipped, each pair of numbers after them is a point (not flipped), and a final Z makes the entity closed. A tag starting with `circle ` starts a kind 1 entity on the current layer with one point of its cx and cy attributes and r from its r attribute. A tag starting with `text ` starts a kind 2 entity on the current layer with one point of its x and y attributes, r from its font-size attribute, rot from the first number inside `rotate(` of its transform attribute when there is one (0 otherwise), and the text that follows the tag up to the next less-than sign, added to `TB` with tx and tn set.

`parse_plt(s, h)` clears `EN`, `EP` and `TB`, sets `PLD` to the number of commands whose text starts with `LT`, and reads the HPGL text s as commands separated by semicolons, each command with the line breaks at its start dropped. It keeps the current layer (minus one at first), the pending point (set by PU), the text height and the text rotation. A plotter unit is 1/40 of a millimetre and y is turned into h minus y. IN does nothing. SP followed by a number n sets the layer to n minus one. PU followed by x,y sets the pending point. PD followed by pairs x,y, comma separated, starts a path entity on the current layer with the pending point first and then every pair; when it has at least 4 points and its last point equals its first it becomes closed and the repeated last point is dropped, and when it is then closed with exactly 24 points that all lie within 0.05 of the same distance (by `pdist`) from their average, it is replaced by a kind 1 entity whose one point is that average and whose r is that distance (the 24 points are removed again from `EP`). SI w,h sets the text height to h times ten. DI c,s sets the text rotation to minus the degrees of the arc tangent of s over c (by `atan2`). LB starts a kind 2 entity on the current layer at the pending point with r the text height, the text rotation, and the characters of the command after LB up to but not including the byte 3, added to `TB` with tx and tn set.

`lstat(layer)` sets `ST` from the entities of `EN` on that layer (every layer when the argument is minus one): `loops` counts closed kind 0 entities, `paths` the open ones, `circs` kind 1, `texts` kind 2; `perim` is the summed perimeter of the closed kind 0 entities; `x0`, `y0`, `x1`, `y1` are the smallest and largest x and y over the points of the kind 0 entities and over the circles' centres moved by plus and minus their r, all 0 when there are none.

`set_garment(g)` calls `apply_preset(2)`, sets `O` to an `Opt` with garment g, fit 1, knit true for garments 0 and 7 and false otherwise, sleeve 0 for garment 2 and 3 for the others, neck 0, skirt 1 and leg 0, and calls `draft_all()`.

`near(a, b, tol)` tells whether a and b differ by at most tol.

`square(sa)` removes every piece with `pieces_clear()`, then adds one piece named "Test" of 10 by 10 centimetres: `tp_clear()`, then `tp_add` of the corners (0,0), (10,0), (10,10) and (0,10) in that order, each with the allowance sa, then `commit` with the note "", a cut of 1 and the grainline from (5,2) to (5,8), and finally `offset_all()`.

`render(out, fmt)` clears out, sets `CU.fmt` to fmt and writes sheet 0 into out with `cu_file`.

`parse_any(fmt, s)` parses the file text s with `parse_dxf` and the height of sheet 0 for fmt 0, with `parse_svg` for fmt 1 and with `parse_plt` and the height of sheet 0 for fmt 2.

`reset_cut()` puts `CU` back to its defaults: fmt 0, sheet 10, cw 304.8, ch 457.2, margin 7, gap 5, grain true, seam true, layers true.

`t_square()` checks the 10 centimetre square in all three formats. For the case seam false and then the case seam true it calls `square(0.5)` and `reset_cut()`, sets `CU.seam` to the case, `CU.layers` to false and `CU.sheet` to 10, calls `cu_build()`, and for each fmt 0, 1 and 2 renders sheet 0 with `render`, parses it back with `parse_any` and takes `lstat(0)`. In both cases there is 1 loop, no open path, no circle and no text, the loop is closed, and the text of the file contains neither `SCORE` nor `ENGRAVE`. With seam false (the cut is the stitch line) the width x1 minus x0 and the height y1 minus y0 are both 100.0 within 0.01 and the perimeter is 400.0 within 0.05. With seam true (the cut is the seam allowance of 0.5 centimetres) they are 110.0 within 0.01 and the perimeter is 440.0 within 0.05. Each check message names the format and the case. For fmt 1 it also checks that `SV.w` and `SV.vw` equal the width of `CUS[0]` and `SV.h` and `SV.vh` its height, within 0.01, and that the width of `CUS[0]` is 914.4 within 0.01.

`t_dxf()` checks the structure of the DXF file of the 10 centimetre square made by `square(0.5)` with `reset_cut()`, seam true and layers true: after `cu_build()` it renders fmt 0 with `render`, and checks that the text starts with "0\nSECTION\n2\nHEADER\n" and ends with "0\nEOF\n" and contains "$INSUNITS". It parses it with `parse_dxf` and h equal to zero (so every y stays as written) and checks `DX.bad` is 0, `DX.sect` equals `DX.endsec` and is 4, `DX.eof` is true, `DX.units` is 4, `DX.acad` is 1009, `DX.layers` is 3, `DX.poly` equals `DX.seq`, and every point of `EP` has y from 0 to the height of `CUS[0]`. It keeps the points of the first closed kind 0 entity on layer 1 (SCORE) as a local vector dp. Then it renders fmt 1, parses it with `parse_svg` and takes the points of the first closed kind 0 entity on layer 1 as sp, and checks that dp and sp have the same number of points, 4, and that for each i the x of both are equal within 0.01 and the y of dp plus the y of sp is the height of `CUS[0]` within 0.01 (the DXF is y up, the SVG is y down). Last, it sets `CU.layers` to false, builds again, renders and parses the DXF again and checks `DX.layers` is 1.

`t_layers()` checks the layers of the 10 centimetre square with an allowance of 0.5 and seam true. With `CU.layers` true, for each fmt: the CUT layer has 1 loop and no text, the SCORE layer has 1 loop (the stitch line, 100 by 100 within 0.01) and the ENGRAVE layer has a grainline path of 2 points plus 2 arrow heads of 3 points (3 open paths, so `paths` is 3 for fmt 1 and fmt 2 and for fmt 0 too) and 1 text that is "Test" (check the characters of `TB` at its tx for its tn; for fmt 2 the text is also "Test"). With `CU.layers` false: only the CUT layer has any entity (`lstat(1)` and `lstat(2)` all zero, and `lstat(-1)` loops 1) and for fmt 1 the file has no `SCORE` and no `ENGRAVE`, and for fmt 2 the text contains no "SP2" and no "SP3". Also check that the SVG written with layers true has `SV.groups` 3 and `SV.ink` 3, `SV.fills` 0 and `SV.dashes` 0 (so no fills and no dashes), and that the PLT has `PLD` 0 and contains "IN;" at its start and "SP1;".

`t_marks()` checks slots and punches. With `reset_cut()`, `CU.layers` true and `CU.seam` true, and the 10 centimetre square with an allowance of 1.0 centimetre and one notch added with `notch` at (5,0) with outward direction (0,-1), it calls `cu_fold(0)` and `cu_prepare(0)`, and checks that `CULP` has 8 points (4 corners plus 4 for the slot), that its bounding box (`cu_bbox`) is 120 by 120 within 0.01, and that the 4 points of the slot (the ones that are not at a corner of that bounding box) have these 6 pairwise distances: two of 2.0 (the slot width `CU_SLOT`), two of 7.0 (the depth `CU_NOTCH`) and two of the square root of 53, all within 0.01. With `CU.seam` false the same piece gives a depth of `CU_NOTCH0` (3.0, within 0.01) and a bounding box of 100 by 100. Then with the square (allowance 0.5, seam true) given a `button` at (3,3), a `dot` at (7,7) and a `dart` with legs (4,10) and (6,10) and apex (5,6), `cu_build()` gives, for sheet 0, exactly 3 primitives of kind `PK_CIRC` on layer `LY_CUT` whose radii are `CU_BTN` once and `CU_HOLE` twice (within 0.001), and the number of `PK_LOOP` primitives on `LY_CUT` is still 1. It parses the DXF, SVG and PLT of that build back and checks that `lstat(0)` gives 3 circles in each, and that their radii are the same in all formats within 0.05.

`dpoly(a, b, c, d)` tells whether the segments a to b and c to d cross properly: the orientations of c and d around the line a to b have opposite strict signs, and so have those of a and b around the line c to d, strict meaning the cross product has an absolute value above 1e-9.

`polys_overlap(f0, n0, f1, n1)` tells whether the closed polygons of the points of `CUP` from f0 for n0 and from f1 for n1 overlap: false at once when their bounding boxes (`cu_bbox`) do not intersect; true when some edge of one properly crosses some edge of the other by `dpoly`; true when the first point of one is inside the other by `cu_inside`; false otherwise.

`check_nest(tag)` checks the nested result of the last `cu_build()`, with tag naming the case in every message. No item has sheet minus three. The width and height of the sheet are the preset's (`CU_PW`, `CU_PH`) within 0.01 for fixed presets other than 12, are `CU.cw` and `CU.ch` for the preset 12, and a roll (`CU.sheet` 10 or 11) gives exactly 1 sheet whose height is at least the margin times two. Every primitive of a sheet lies in the rectangle from 0 to its width and from 0 to its height, and every point of the `PK_LOOP` primitives on `LY_CUT` lies at least `CU.margin` minus 0.01 from each side. No two `PK_LOOP` primitives on `LY_CUT` of the same sheet overlap, by `polys_overlap`. For every piece k and every copy c below the larger of the piece's cut and 1, at least one item with sheet zero or more has that piece and copy, and the copies field of those items is that count; when `CU.grain` is true every placed item whose grain is true has rot 0 or 2.

`t_nest()` runs `check_nest` over the nested result of every garment g below 8 (drafted with `set_garment(g)`), every sheet preset from 0 to 12 (preset 12 with `CU.cw` 500 and `CU.ch` 700), `CU.grain` true and false, and the margin and gap pairs (7, 5) and (20, 12) (for the garments 0 and 4 only; the other garments use (7, 5) only). Each tag is like "T-shirt preset 3 grain 1 m7 g5". It also checks that, for the T-shirt and for the trousers on preset 10 with grain true, the sum of the item areas of the placed items divided by the sheet area (width times height) is between 0.4 and 0.9, and that on preset 10 there are no tiled items. It counts how many nests were checked and how many sheets and tiles were seen in total, and prints them.

`t_tiles()` checks the splitting of oversize pieces for the trousers (`set_garment(4)`) on preset 3 (A4) with `CU.grain` true, margin 7 and gap 5. After `cu_build()` the usable width uw is the sheet width minus twice the margin minus `CU_CW`, the usable height uh is the sheet height minus twice the margin and ov is `cu_ov(uw, uh)`. There is at least one tiled item. Every tiled item has w at most uw plus 0.01 and h at most uh plus 0.01. For each piece k and copy c that has tiled items, the unplaced source item (the one with that piece and copy, tiled false and sheet minus two) has the width sw and height sh, and the points of the `PK_LOOP` primitive at `cut` of each of those tiled items, moved by plus the item's ox and oy, reach together from x 0 to sw and from y 0 to sh within 0.001 (their smallest x and y are 0 and their largest x and y are sw and sh). For two tiled items of the same piece and copy and the same tr whose tc differ by one, the difference of their ox is uw minus ov within 0.001. Registration: for every tiled item and every text primitive on `LY_SCORE` of that item (the primitives from its first for its n) whose text holds a slash, the same text appears the same number of times in the other tile of that pair (the pair is the two tile names of `cu_tile_id` around the slash, one of them being this item's own tile) with the same positions, so that for every such text at the point q of the first item there is a text of that string in the second item whose point plus its own ox and oy equals q plus the first item's ox and oy within 0.001; and the cross centre of that text (its point minus 4 in x and plus 2 in y) lies inside the item's own `PK_LOOP` at `cut` by `cu_inside`. With layers false (`CU.layers` set false and `cu_build()` again) the tiled items have no text primitive on `LY_SCORE`.

`t_names()` checks names and presets. With `CUS` holding 3 `CuSheet` entries (each `CuSheet { w: 1.0, h: 1.0, roll: false, used: 0.0, sky: 0, count: 0 }`) and `CU.fmt` 0, 1, 2 in turn, `cu_filename` with the stem "amni-stitch-t-shirt" and sheet 1 writes "amni-stitch-t-shirt-cut-sheet-2-of-3.dxf", then the same with ".svg", then with ".plt". `cu_mime()` is "image/vnd.dxf", "image/svg+xml" and "application/vnd.hp-HPGL" for the three formats, and `cu_ext()` is "dxf", "svg" and "plt" (without a dot) for the three formats. After `square(0.5)`, `reset_cut()` and `cu_build()`, the text that `cu_info` appends to an empty vector starts with "1 sheet " and contains "% of the material used"; after `CUS.clear()` it is "Nothing to cut". The presets: for each preset below 12 `cu_dims()` returns `CU_PW` and `CU_PH` of that preset; for preset 12 it returns `CU.cw` and `CU.ch`; `CU_PW` of preset 0 is 215.9, of preset 5 is 594.0 and `CU_PH` of preset 6 is 1189.0, and `CU_PRESETS` is 13 and the number of `CU_NAMES` is 13.

`texts_of(out)` appends to out the characters of every kind 2 entity of `EN`, in order, each followed by a vertical bar.

`t_files()` checks that the three formats agree for the T-shirt and for the trousers (garments 0 and 4, drafted with `set_garment`) on preset 10 with `CU.grain` true and layers true, by building once, rendering and parsing each of the three formats and taking `lstat(0)`, `lstat(1)` and `lstat(2)` for each. For each layer the number of loops, open paths, circles and texts equal those of the SVG for the DXF and the PLT (the PLT has the same counts as the SVG), the perimeter of the DXF and the PLT equals the SVG's within 0.5 plus 0.0005 times the SVG's, and the bounding box corners equal within 0.2. They also equal the counts taken straight from `CUR` for sheet 0: the number of `PK_LOOP` primitives, `PK_PATH` primitives, `PK_CIRC` and `PK_TEXT` primitives on each layer. The strings of `texts_of` of the DXF and of the PLT equal that of the SVG. Print the case name and the counts.

`main()` runs `t_square()`, `t_dxf()`, `t_layers()`, `t_marks()`, `t_nest()`, `t_tiles()`, `t_names()` and `t_files()`, prints "cut checks " then the number of checks `CHK` and " failures " then `FAIL`, and exits with status 1 when `FAIL` is above zero.
